# The lock screen's password check, used when the system has no
# /etc/pam.d/h2s-shell-password (scripts/lock-auth-methods.sh says which config is used).
#
# The local password (/etc/shadow, through pam_unix and its setuid helper unix_chkpwd)
# and nothing else: nothing that waits for a fingerprint or a security key — those have
# conversations of their own, and a password check that waited for them would hold up the
# typed password. Accounts kept elsewhere (systemd-homed, LDAP/SSSD) need a system-wide
# h2s-shell-password: see the README.
#
# Quickshell runs pam_authenticate and nothing else, so only `auth` lines mean anything
# here: no account, session or password phase ever runs (an expired account is not refused
# for being expired).
#
# No `nullok`: an account without a password is not unlocked by whatever is typed.
# The first line only says that PAM got going (pam_echo's message, never shown, changes no
# result): libpam loads every module before it runs, and a check that has not said it
# within a few seconds is stuck before any module ran, and is started again. Keep it first.
auth     [default=ignore] pam_echo.so h2s-shell-lock.started
auth     required pam_unix.so
